  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a rumour?",
      "a": "A rumour is a story or piece of information that people pass on to each other without knowing whether it is true. Some rumours are true, but many are wrong or changed along the way.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about Zara starts it",
        "Rumour meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do rumours spread faster online?",
      "a": "One message in a group chat or a post can reach many people at once, and they can share it again with one tap. So a rumour can reach thousands in minutes.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about group chat",
        "Do phones spread rumours faster?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can a rumour reach a whole school so quickly?",
      "a": "Because each person tells several others, the number of people who know keeps multiplying, not just adding. In a few rounds it reaches almost everyone.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about whole school",
        "How does a rumour travel?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does doubling make rumours grow so fast?",
      "a": "If each person tells two new people, the count goes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 and so on. After 10 rounds, 1,024 people could know.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about round 6",
        "What is exponential growth?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What should I do if I hear a rumour?",
      "a": "Pause before passing it on. Ask: is it true, is it kind, is it necessary? If it could hurt someone, do not share it, and if someone is being hurt, tell a trusted adult.",
      "asks": [
        "Should I pass on a rumour?",
        "How do I stop a rumour?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A 2018 MIT study by Vosoughi, Roy and Aral found false stories on Twitter were about 70 percent more likely to be retweeted than true ones.",
    "Doubling ten times turns 1 into 1,024."
